          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-hh-dihedral-root-recurrence-and-root-sign",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "1. **Involutions.** $\\sigma_s^2=\\mathrm{id}_E$ for every $s\\in S$; each $\\sigma_s$ is invertible, and $\\ell(s)=1$.\n2. **The rank-two block.** For any distinct $s,t\\in S$, put $m:=m(s,t)$, $c:=c_{st}$, $P:=\\operatorname{span}(\\alpha_s,\\alpha_t)\\subseteq E$, $A:=\\sigma_s\\sigma_t$ and $B:=A|_P$. Then $A(P)\\subseteq P$, and in the basis $(\\alpha_s,\\alpha_t)$,\n$$B(\\alpha_s)=(c^2-1)\\alpha_s+c\\,\\alpha_t,\\qquad B(\\alpha_t)=-c\\,\\alpha_s-\\alpha_t;\\qquad \\operatorname{tr}(B)=c^2-2,\\qquad \\det(B)=1 .$$\nMoreover $A(v)-v\\in P$ for every $v\\in E$. Consequently $E=P\\oplus Q$ with $Q:=\\operatorname{span}\\{\\alpha_u:u\\in S,\\ u\\notin\\{s,t\\}\\}$, and for every $v=p+q$ with $p\\in P$, $q\\in Q$ and every $k\\ge1$,\n$$A^k(p+q)=B^k p+\\bigl(\\mathrm{id}_P+B+\\cdots+B^{k-1}\\bigr)\\bigl(A(q)-q\\bigr)+q .$$\n3. **Exact order of the rank-two product.** For any distinct $s,t$ and the notation of (2), when $m<\\infty$ put $\\zeta:=\\zeta_{st}$, of order $2m$, so $c=\\zeta+\\zeta^{-1}$; when $m=\\infty$ use $c=2$, with no $\\zeta$ stipulated. Then:\n   (a) if $2<m<\\infty$, the characteristic polynomial of $B$ is $X^2-(c^2-2)X+1=(X-\\zeta^2)(X-\\zeta^{-2})$ with distinct roots; $B$ is diagonalisable, $B^m=\\mathrm{id}_P$, $\\mathrm{id}_P+B+\\cdots+B^{m-1}=0$, and $B^k\\ne\\mathrm{id}_P$ for $0<k<m$;\n   (b) if $m=2$, then $c=0$ and $B=-\\mathrm{id}_P$;\n   (c) if $m=\\infty$, then $c=2$, $B\\ne\\mathrm{id}_P$, $(B-\\mathrm{id}_P)^2=0$, and $B^k\\ne\\mathrm{id}_P$ for every $k\\ge1$.\n   Hence $\\sigma_s\\sigma_t=A$ has order exactly $m$ when $m<\\infty$, and infinite order when $m=\\infty$; in particular $A^m=\\mathrm{id}_E$ and $A^k\\ne\\mathrm{id}_E$ for $0<k<m$ in the finite case.\n4. **The representation and the exact dihedral orders.** The assignment $s\\mapsto\\sigma_s$ respects every defining relator of $W$ and induces a unique homomorphism $\\sigma:W\\to\\mathrm{GL}_K(E)$ with $\\sigma(s)=\\sigma_s$. Consequently, for any distinct $s,t\\in S$, one has $s\\ne t$ in $W$ and $st$ has order exactly $m(s,t)$ in $W$ (infinite when $m(s,t)=\\infty$).",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.4",
            "1.5",
            "5.1",
            "6.1",
            "8.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-hh-coxeter-exchange-deletion-and-faithfulness",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(S,m)$ be a finite Coxeter matrix, $W$ the presented group, $\\ell$ its length function, and let $T$, $\\eta$, $\\Phi$ and the right action of $W$ on $\\{\\pm1\\}\\times T$ be as in [[lem-hh-dihedral-root-recurrence-and-root-sign]].\n\n1. **Sign character and parity.** There is a unique homomorphism $\\operatorname{sgn}:W\\to\\{\\pm1\\}$ with $\\operatorname{sgn}(s)=-1$ for all $s\\in S$, and $\\operatorname{sgn}(w)=(-1)^{\\ell(w)}$ for all $w\\in W$. Consequently, for all $w\\in W$ and $s\\in S$,\n$$\\ell(sw)=\\ell(w)\\pm1,\\qquad \\ell(ws)=\\ell(w)\\pm1,$$\nwith $\\ell(sw)\\equiv\\ell(w)+1\\pmod2$ and $\\ell(ws)\\equiv\\ell(w)+1\\pmod2$.\n2. **Exchange.** Let $w=s_1\\cdots s_k$ be a reduced expression and let $s\\in S$ satisfy $\\ell(sw)=k-1$. Then\n$$sw=s_1\\cdots\\widehat{s_i}\\cdots s_k$$\nfor some $i\\in\\{1,\\dots,k\\}$; equivalently, $w$ has a reduced expression beginning with $s$, and $s$ is a prefix reflection of any reduced expression of $w$. Right-handed form: if $\\ell(ws)=k-1$ then $ws=s_1\\cdots\\widehat{s_i}\\cdots s_k$ for some $i$.\n3. **Deletion.** If the word $(s_1,\\dots,s_k)$ in $S$ is not reduced, then there are $i<j$ with\n$$s_1\\cdots\\widehat{s_i}\\cdots\\widehat{s_j}\\cdots s_k=s_1\\cdots s_k .$$\nHence repeated deletion of two letters transforms every word into a reduced expression for the same element, and a word is reduced if and only if it cannot be shortened by deleting two letters.\n4. **Faithfulness of the signed action.** The right action of $W$ on $\\{\\pm1\\}\\times T$ is faithful, so $W$ embeds in $\\operatorname{Sym}(\\{\\pm1\\}\\times T)$; in particular distinct simple generators are distinct in $W$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.4",
            "1.5",
            "5.1",
            "6.1",
            "8.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-hh-parabolic-minimal-representatives-and-length-additivity",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(S,m)$ be a finite Coxeter matrix with presented group $W$, length $\\ell$ and geometric representation $\\sigma:W\\to\\mathrm{GL}_K(E)$ as in [[def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length]], [[def-hh-geometric-coxeter-representation-and-roots]] and [[lem-hh-dihedral-root-recurrence-and-root-sign]], and let $J\\subseteq S$.\n\n1. **Support and reduction.** For every $w\\in W$ the set $S(w)$ of letters occurring in a reduced expression of $w$ is independent of the reduced expression, and every word in $S$ representing $w$ can be transformed into a reduced expression by repeatedly deleting two letters (Tits reduction, using only letters already present). Consequently\n$$W_J=\\langle J\\rangle=\\{w\\in W:S(w)\\subseteq J\\}.$$\n2. **Intrinsic parabolic presentation.** Let $W_J^\\ast$ be the group presented by the restricted Coxeter matrix $(J,m|_J)$ in the sense of [[def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length]]. The canonical homomorphism $W_J^\\ast\\to W_J$, $s\\mapsto s$, is an isomorphism. Hence $(W_J,J)$ is a Coxeter system, its intrinsic length function $\\ell_J$ agrees with the ambient length $\\ell$ on $W_J$, and $W_J\\cap S=J$.\n3. **Minimal coset representatives.** Every left coset $W_J a:=\\{ua:u\\in W_J\\}$ ($a\\in W$, [[def-coset]]) has a unique element $d$ of minimal length; it is characterized by $\\ell(sd)>\\ell(d)$ for all $s\\in J$, and it satisfies\n$$\\ell(ud)=\\ell(u)+\\ell(d)\\qquad\\text{for all }u\\in W_J .$$\nEquivalently, every $w\\in W$ has a unique factorization $w=u\\,d$ with $u\\in W_J$ and $d$ the minimal representative of the left coset $W_J d$, and then $\\ell(w)=\\ell(u)+\\ell(d)$. By inversion ($w\\mapsto w^{-1}$ preserves lengths and interchanges the two coset families $\\{W_Ja\\}$ and $\\{aW_J\\}$), every right coset $aW_J:=\\{au:u\\in W_J\\}$ has a unique minimal element $d$, characterized by $\\ell(ds)>\\ell(d)$ for all $s\\in J$ and satisfying $\\ell(du)=\\ell(d)+\\ell(u)$ for all $u\\in W_J$.\n4. **Type A.** Let $n\\ge2$, $S=\\{s_1,\\dots,s_{n-1}\\}$ and $m(s_i,s_j):=3$ if $|i-j|=1$, $m(s_i,s_j):=2$ if $|i-j|>1$ (a Coxeter matrix of type $A_{n-1}$). Then $s_i\\mapsto(i\\ i+1)$ extends to an isomorphism $W\\to S_n$ (the letters $1,\\dots,n$ carry the library's symmetric group by the order-preserving identification with $\\{0,\\dots,n-1\\}$, under which $(i\\ i+1)$ is the adjacent transposition $(i-1\\ i)$), and for every $w\\in W$,\n$$\\ell(w)=\\operatorname{inv}\\bigl(\\varphi(w)\\bigr),$$\nthe inversion number of the corresponding permutation ([[def-inversions-inversion-number-and-sign]]). In particular a word in the $s_i$ is reduced if and only if its length equals the inversion number of its value.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.4",
            "1.5",
            "5.1",
            "6.1",
            "8.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "lem-hh-dihedral-root-recurrence-and-root-sign",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "7. **Ambient reducedness of dihedral words.** Let $s\\ne t$, $m:=m(s,t)$, and for $q\\ge1$ let $w_q$ be the value of the alternating word of length $q$ beginning with $s$. If $m<\\infty$ and $q\\le m$, or if $m=\\infty$ and $q\\ge1$, then $\\ell(w_q)=q$, every word in $S$ representing $w_q$ has length at least $q$, and $w_1,\\dots,w_q$ are pairwise distinct. Thus dihedral alternating words are reduced in the ambient group $W$, not merely inside $\\langle s,t\\rangle$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.4",
            "1.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-induction-principle",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{N}$. If $0 \\in S$ and $\\sigma(n) \\in S$ whenever $n \\in S$, then $S = \\mathbb{N}$. Equivalently (property form): if a property $P$ satisfies $P(0)$ and ($P(n) \\Rightarrow P(\\sigma(n))$) for all $n$, then $P(n)$ holds for all $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$. This is the induction principle, the basis of proof by induction.",
          "uses": [
            "7.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "**Set-up.** For the rank-two calculation fix $s\\ne t$ in $S$ and adopt the notation of [F1] and [F3]. For $r\\in S$ the sets $B_r=\\{f\\in V^*:f(e_r)>0\\}$ and $rB_r=\\{f\\in V^*:f(e_r)<0\\}$ are disjoint and $C^\\circ=\\bigcap_{r\\in S}B_r$; the dual action satisfies $v(B_s\\cap B_t)=\\{v\\cdot f:f\\in B_s\\cap B_t\\}$, and the action of $v\\in W_{s,t}$ on $V^*$ is linear. By [F5] the intrinsic length satisfies $\\ell_{s,t}(x)=\\ell(x)$ for $x\\in W_{s,t}$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "F5",
            "given"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "**Reduction to the rank-two plane.** Put $C_P^\\circ:=\\{g\\in P^*:g(e_s)>0,\\ g(e_t)>0\\}$ and let $\\pi:V^*\\to P^*$, $\\pi(f):=f|_P$. Then $\\pi$ is linear and surjective, because a functional prescribed on $P$ extends to a functional on $V$ by $0$ on the remaining basis vectors. Every $v\\in W_{s,t}$ preserves $P$ and fixes $P^\\perp$ pointwise by [F1], so for $x\\in P$ and $f\\in V^*$ one has $(v\\cdot f)(x)=f(\\rho(v)^{-1}x)$ with $\\rho(v)^{-1}x\\in P$; hence $\\pi(v\\cdot f)=v\\cdot\\pi(f)$ and $\\pi^{-1}(C_P^\\circ)=B_s\\cap B_t$, $\\pi^{-1}\\{g:g(e_{s'})>0\\}=B_{s'}$, $\\pi^{-1}\\{g:g(e_{s'})<0\\}=s'B_{s'}$ for $s'\\in\\{s,t\\}$. Consequently $v(B_s\\cap B_t)\\subseteq B_{s'}$ if and only if $vC_P^\\circ\\subseteq\\{g:g(e_{s'})>0\\}$, and $v(B_s\\cap B_t)\\subseteq s'B_{s'}$ if and only if $vC_P^\\circ\\subseteq\\{g:g(e_{s'})<0\\}$.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-3",
          "claim": "**Sectors avoid the walls.** By [F4] the interiors of the chambers $wC_P$ $(w\\in W_{s,t})$ are convex open cones that are connected components of the complement of the union of the root lines in $P^*$ (respectively in $\\{f\\in P^*:f(e_s+e_t)>0\\}$ when $m(s,t)=\\infty$), so each is disjoint from every root line $H_\\beta\\cap P^*$ $(\\beta\\in\\Phi_P)$, and $H_{e_s}\\cap P^*$, $H_{e_t}\\cap P^*$ are root lines because $e_s,e_t\\in\\Phi_P$. An open convex set disjoint from a line lies in exactly one of the two open half-planes bounded by it. Hence for every $v\\in W_{s,t}$ and $s'\\in\\{s,t\\}$ exactly one of $vC_P^\\circ\\subseteq\\{g:g(e_{s'})>0\\}$ and $vC_P^\\circ\\subseteq\\{g:g(e_{s'})<0\\}$ holds.",
          "step": "1.3",
          "inputs": [
            "F4",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-4",
          "claim": "**The finite dihedral group.** Assume $m:=m(s,t)<\\infty$ and write $u:=st$. Since $u^m=1$ by [F5], the set $\\{u_0,\\dots,u_{2m-1}\\}$ is closed under right multiplication by $s$ and $t$: indeed $u_{2j}s=u_{2j+1}$ and $u_{2j+1}s=u_{2j}$ for $2j<2m$; $u_{2j}t=u_{2j-1}$ for $j\\ge1$; $u_0t=u_{2m-1}$ because $(st)^{m-1}s=(st)^{-1}s=(ts)s=t$; and $u_{2j+1}t=u_{2j+2}$ for $2j+1<2m$, $u_{2m-1}t=1$. Since $W_{s,t}$ is generated by $s,t$ and contains $1$, it equals $\\{u_0,\\dots,u_{2m-1}\\}$. The $u_k$ are pairwise distinct: if $u_j=u_k$ with $j<k<2m$, then $1=u_j^{-1}u_k$; for $j,k$ of equal parity this equals $u^{\\pm(k-j)/2}$ with $0<(k-j)/2<m$, contradicting the exact order $m$ of $u$ from [F5], while for parities differing it equals $s\\,u^{p}$ or $u^{p}s$ with $2|p|<2m$, so it would give $u^{p}=s$, impossible because $\\operatorname{sgn}(u^{p})=1$ while $\\operatorname{sgn}(s)=-1$. Hence $|W_{s,t}|=2m$, the $u_k$ are distinct, $u_{k+2m}=u_k$, and the pairs $u_k,u_{k+1}$ and $u_{2m-1},u_0$ are edges of the Cayley graph of $W_{s,t}$ for the generating set $\\{s,t\\}$. That graph is connected, and every vertex has degree exactly $2$ because $x\\ne xs$ and $xs\\ne xt$ for all $x$ (as $s\\ne1$ and $s\\ne t$ by [F5]); it therefore *is* the displayed $2m$-cycle, so $\\ell_{s,t}(u_k)=\\min(k,2m-k)$ is the graph distance from $u_0$. Moreover $su_k=u_{\\sigma(k)}$ and $tu_k=u_{\\tau(k)}$ with $\\sigma(k)\\equiv1-k\\pmod{2m}$ and $\\tau(k)\\equiv-1-k\\pmod{2m}$ for $0\\le k<2m$: these are $s(st)^{j}=u^{-j}s$, $t(st)^{j}=u^{-j}t=u^{-j-1}s$ applied to $u_{2j}=u^{j}$ and $u_{2j+1}=u^{j}s$, together with the case $k=0$.",
          "step": "1.4",
          "inputs": [
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-5",
          "claim": "**The infinite dihedral group.** Assume $m(s,t)=\\infty$, write $u:=st$, and let $u'_k$ be the alternating word of length $k$ beginning with $t$. By [F5], $u$ has infinite order, and by [F6] $\\ell_{s,t}(u_k)=k=\\ell_{s,t}(u'_k)$ for all $k\\ge0$. The union $X:=\\{u_k:k\\ge0\\}\\cup\\{u'_k:k\\ge0\\}$ is closed under right multiplication by $s$ and $t$ ($u_{2j}s=u_{2j+1}$, $u_{2j+1}s=u_{2j}$, $u_{2j}t=u_{2j-1}$ for $j\\ge1$, $u_0t=u'_1$, $u_{2j+1}t=u_{2j+2}$, symmetrically for $u'_k$), so $X=W_{s,t}$; by [F6] the elements $u_k$ are pairwise distinct with $\\ell_{s,t}(u_k)=k$ for every $k\\ge0$, and likewise the $u'_k$. The Cayley graph of $W_{s,t}$ for $\\{s,t\\}$ is connected, and every vertex has degree exactly $2$ (as in 1.4), so it is the two-way infinite path $\\cdots u'_2,u'_1,1,u_1,u_2\\cdots$, and the graph distance from $1$ to $u_k$, or to $u'_k$, equals $\\ell_{s,t}$ of that element, namely $k$. On the affine line $\\{f\\in P^*:f(e_s+e_t)=1\\}$, parametrized by $y_s$, the generators act by $s:y_s\\mapsto-y_s$ and $t:y_s\\mapsto 2-y_s$ by [F3] (here $c=1$); consequently the affine map of $u_k$ is $y_s\\mapsto y_s-2j$ for $k=2j$ and $y_s\\mapsto-y_s-2j$ for $k=2j+1$, and that of $u'_k$ is $y_s\\mapsto y_s+2j$ for $k=2j$ and $y_s\\mapsto-y_s+2j+2$ for $k=2j+1$, by induction on $k$ (each step composes with $s$ or $t$ alternately). Hence the trace of $u_kC_P^\\circ$ is the interval $(-k,-k+1)$ and that of $u'_kC_P^\\circ$ is $(k,k+1)$. Since these intervals are pairwise distinct, an element of $W_{s,t}=X$ is determined by the trace of its chamber. Writing $v_j$ $(j\\in\\mathbb Z)$ for the element with trace $(j,j+1)$, namely $v_j=u_{-j}$ for $j\\le0$ and $v_j=u'_j$ for $j\\ge0$, one therefore has $\\ell_{s,t}(v_j)=|j|$ together with $s\\cdot v_j=v_{-j-1}$ and $t\\cdot v_j=v_{1-j}$, because the affine maps send the interval $(j,j+1)$ to $(-j-1,-j)$, respectively $(1-j,2-j)$.",
          "step": "1.5",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "algebra",
            "step 1.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-6",
          "claim": "**Signs in the infinite case.** In the situation of 1.5 the sign of $y_s$ on $v_jC_P^\\circ$ is the sign of $y_s$ on the interval $(j,j+1)$, hence negative exactly when $j\\le-1$, and the sign of $y_t=1-y_s$ is negative exactly when $j\\ge1$.",
          "step": "1.6",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "algebra",
            "step 1.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-7",
          "claim": "**(P_0) and (Q_0).** For $w=1$ one has $\\ell(w)=0$, $wC^\\circ=C^\\circ\\subseteq B_r$ for every $r\\in S$ by 1.1, and choosing $u=1$ gives $C^\\circ\\subseteq B_s\\cap B_t$ and $\\ell(1)=0=\\ell_{s,t}(1)+\\ell(1)$ for all $s\\ne t$.",
          "step": "1.7",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "given",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "**Signs of the finite sectors.** In the finite case let $C_k:=u_kC_P^\\circ$ $(0\\le k<2m)$; by [F4] these are the interiors of all $2m$ sectors. Consecutive $u_k$ differ by right multiplication by a generator $r$, so $u_kC_P$ and $u_krC_P$ share the image under $u_k$ of the wall of $C_P$ fixed by $r$ and lie on its opposite sides, by [F3]. They are adjacent sectors. The distinct vertices of the Cayley cycle in 1.4 therefore list the sectors in cyclic order. The sign of $y_s$ is constant on each $C_k$ (no $C_k$ meets the line $y_s=0$ by 1.3), is $+$ on $C_0$, and is $-$ on $C_1=sC_P^\\circ$ because $s$ negates $y_s$ by [F3]. It changes exactly at those transitions whose common boundary ray lies on the line $y_s=0$: that line contains exactly two boundary rays of the arrangement, hence exactly two transitions; and the involution $s$ maps each sector to the sector on the opposite side of $y_s=0$ and fixes no sector, because $s$ swaps the two half-planes and no sector meets the wall. Hence exactly $m$ sectors lie on each side, and the negative side is the contiguous block of $m$ sectors containing $C_1$ but not $C_0$, that is $\\{k:y_s<0\\text{ on }C_k\\}=\\{1,\\dots,m\\}$. Applying the same argument to $t$, which is $u_{2m-1}$ and negates $y_t$, gives $\\{k:y_t<0\\text{ on }C_k\\}=\\{m,\\dots,2m-1\\}$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "step 1.3",
            "step 1.4",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "claim": "**Descents in the infinite case.** In the situation of 1.5, let $v=v_j$ be the element with trace $(j,j+1)$; by 1.5, $\\ell_{s,t}(v)=|j|$ and $\\ell_{s,t}(sv)=|j+1|$, so $\\ell_{s,t}(sv)=\\ell_{s,t}(v)-1$ exactly when $j\\le-1$, which by 1.6 is exactly the side $y_s<0$; and $\\ell_{s,t}(tv)=|j-1|$, so $\\ell_{s,t}(tv)=\\ell_{s,t}(v)-1$ exactly when $j\\ge1$, exactly the side $y_t<0$. In particular no element has both left descents, and $\\ell_{s,t}(u_k)=k$ for all $k\\ge0$ with the $u_k$ pairwise distinct.",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.5",
            "step 1.6",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-3",
          "claim": "**The dichotomy in the original space.** Combining 1.2 with 1.3: for every $v\\in W_{s,t}$ and $s'\\in\\{s,t\\}$ exactly one of $v(B_s\\cap B_t)\\subseteq B_{s'}$ and $v(B_s\\cap B_t)\\subseteq s'B_{s'}$ holds, according to the side of $H_{e_{s'}}\\cap P^*$ carrying $vC_P^\\circ$.",
          "step": "2.3",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.2",
            "step 1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "**Descents in the finite case.** Let $m(s,t)<\\infty$ and $v=u_k$ $(0\\le k<2m)$. By 1.4, $\\ell_{s,t}(su_k)$ equals the distance from $0$ to $\\sigma(k)\\equiv1-k$, namely $k-1$ when $1\\le k\\le m$ and $2m+1-k$ when $m<k<2m$, and equals $1$ when $k=0$; comparing with $\\ell_{s,t}(u_k)=\\min(k,2m-k)$ gives $\\ell_{s,t}(su_k)=\\ell_{s,t}(u_k)-1$ exactly for $1\\le k\\le m$. Likewise $\\ell_{s,t}(tu_k)=\\ell_{s,t}(u_k)-1$ exactly for $m\\le k\\le2m-1$, and $\\ell_{s,t}(tu_k)=\\ell_{s,t}(u_k)+1$ for $0\\le k<m$. Hence, by 2.1, the left descent for $s$ occurs exactly on the side $y_s<0$ and the left descent for $t$ exactly on the side $y_t<0$, and $u_m$ is the unique element with both descents.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.4",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "**Part (1).** Let $v\\in W_{s,t}$ and $s'\\in\\{s,t\\}$. The dichotomy is 2.3, and its two alternatives correspond to the signs of $y_{s'}$ on $vC_P^\\circ$. If $m(s,t)<\\infty$ then $v=u_k$ for a unique $k$ by 1.4 and $\\ell_{s,t}(u_k)=\\min(k,2m-k)$; the negative side alternative holds exactly for $k\\in\\{1,\\dots,m\\}$ when $s'=s$ and for $k\\in\\{m,\\dots,2m-1\\}$ when $s'=t$ by 2.1, and exactly there $s'v$ shortens by 3.1; the elements $u_0,\\dots,u_{2m-1}$ exhaust $W_{s,t}$, and the unique double-descent element is $u_m$. If $m(s,t)=\\infty$ then every $v$ has a trace $(j,j+1)$ and $\\ell_{s,t}(u_k)=k$ with the $u_k$ pairwise distinct by 2.2; the negative side alternative for $s'$ holds exactly for $j\\le-1$ (respectively $j\\ge1$), and exactly there $s'v$ shortens by 2.2, and no element has both descents. This proves all clauses of (1) in both cases.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.4",
            "step 1.5",
            "step 3.1",
            "step 2.2",
            "step 2.3",
            "step 2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "claim": "**The conditional step for $(\\mathrm P)$. ** Fix $n\\ge0$ and suppose only $(\\mathrm Q_n)$. If $S$ is empty there is no $s$ to check. If $S=\\{s\\}$ then $W=\\{1,s\\}$ by $s^2=1$ and [F5], and the only positive-length chamber is $sC^\\circ\\subseteq sB_s$, with $\\ell(s)=1$, proving $(\\mathrm P_{n+1})$ directly. Now suppose $|S|\\ge2$. First $(\\mathrm Q_n)$ and (1) imply $(\\mathrm P_n)$: for any $a$ of length $n$ and generator $r$, choose $q\\ne r$ and use $(\\mathrm Q_n)$ to write $a=ud$, with $aC^\\circ\\subseteq u(B_r\\cap B_q)$ and $n=\\ell_{r,q}(u)+\\ell(d)$. The rank-two dichotomy gives the positive alternative or the negative alternative with $\\ell_{r,q}(ru)=\\ell_{r,q}(u)-1$; in the latter case $\\ell(ra)\\le\\ell_{r,q}(ru)+\\ell(d)=n-1$, hence equality by [F5]. This is $(\\mathrm P_n)$. Let $w\\in W$ with $\\ell(w)=n+1$ and let $s\\in S$; choose $t$ with $w=tw'$ and $\\ell(w')=n$. If $s=t$, then $(\\mathrm P_n)$ applied to $w'$ gives $w'C^\\circ\\subseteq B_s$: the second alternative would give $\\ell(sw')=\\ell(w')-1=n-1$, whereas $\\ell(sw')=\\ell(w)=n+1$ here. Hence $wC^\\circ=t(w'C^\\circ)\\subseteq tB_s=sB_s$ and $\\ell(sw)=\\ell(w')=n=\\ell(w)-1$, the second alternative of $(\\mathrm P_{n+1})$. If $s\\ne t$, apply $(\\mathrm Q_n)$ to $w'$: there is $u\\in W_{s,t}$ with $w'C^\\circ\\subseteq u(B_s\\cap B_t)$ and $\\ell(w')=\\ell_{s,t}(u)+\\ell(u^{-1}w')$. Put $v:=tu\\in W_{s,t}$. If $v(B_s\\cap B_t)\\subseteq B_s$, then $wC^\\circ\\subseteq tu(B_s\\cap B_t)\\subseteq B_s$. Otherwise $v(B_s\\cap B_t)\\subseteq sB_s$ and $\\ell_{s,t}(sv)=\\ell_{s,t}(v)-1$ by (1) from 4.1, so with $d:=u^{-1}w'$ one has $wC^\\circ\\subseteq sB_s$ and $\\ell(sw)=\\ell(stu\\,d)\\le\\ell_{s,t}(stu)+\\ell(d)=\\ell_{s,t}(sv)-1+\\ell(d)\\le\\ell_{s,t}(u)+\\ell(d)=\\ell(w')=\\ell(w)-1$, while $\\ell(sw)\\ge\\ell(w)-1$ by [F5]; hence $\\ell(sw)=\\ell(w)-1$, the second alternative of $(\\mathrm P_{n+1})$.",
          "step": "5.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 4.1",
            "F3",
            "F5",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-6-1",
          "claim": "**The conditional step for $(\\mathrm Q)$. ** Suppose $(\\mathrm Q_n)$ and $(\\mathrm P_m)$ for all $m\\le n+1$, as in the second conditional implication of (2), and let $w\\in W$ with $\\ell(w)=n+1$ and $s\\ne t$. By [F5] factor $w=u\\,d$ with $u\\in W_{s,t}$ and $d$ the minimal representative of $W_{s,t}w$; then $\\ell(w)=\\ell(u)+\\ell(d)$ and $\\ell(s'd)>\\ell(d)$ for $s'\\in\\{s,t\\}$. Since $\\ell(d)\\le n+1$, $(\\mathrm P_{\\ell(d)})$ is available: it is part of the stated supposition. Applying it to $d$ and $s'\\in\\{s,t\\}$, the second alternative would give $\\ell(s'd)=\\ell(d)-1$, contradicting $\\ell(s'd)>\\ell(d)$; hence $dC^\\circ\\subseteq B_s\\cap B_t$. Therefore $wC^\\circ=u(dC^\\circ)\\subseteq u(B_s\\cap B_t)$ and $\\ell(w)=\\ell(u)+\\ell(d)=\\ell_{s,t}(u)+\\ell(u^{-1}w)$, which is $(\\mathrm Q_{n+1})$.",
          "step": "6.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 5.1",
            "F5",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-7-1",
          "claim": "**The induction.** $(\\mathrm P_0)$ and $(\\mathrm Q_0)$ hold by 1.7, and 5.1 and 6.1 show that the validity of $(\\mathrm P_m)$ and $(\\mathrm Q_m)$ for all $m\\le n$ implies $(\\mathrm P_{n+1})$ and $(\\mathrm Q_{n+1})$, for every $n\\ge0$. By the induction principle [F7], $(\\mathrm P_n)$ and $(\\mathrm Q_n)$ hold for every $n\\ge0$.",
          "step": "7.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.7",
            "step 5.1",
            "step 6.1",
            "F7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-8-1",
          "claim": "**Part (3).** Let $w\\in W$ and $s\\ne t$, and factor $w=u\\,d$ with $u\\in W_{s,t}$ and $d$ the minimal representative of $W_{s,t}w$. By [F5], $\\ell(w)=\\ell(u)+\\ell(d)$ and $\\ell(s'd)>\\ell(d)$ for $s'\\in\\{s,t\\}$; since $\\ell(d)\\le\\ell(w)$, $(\\mathrm P_{\\ell(d)})$ from 7.1 applies to $d$, and the second alternative would give $\\ell(s'd)=\\ell(d)-1$, a contradiction; hence $dC^\\circ\\subseteq B_s\\cap B_t$ and $wC^\\circ=u(dC^\\circ)\\subseteq u(B_s\\cap B_t)$, with $\\ell(w)=\\ell(u)+\\ell(d)=\\ell_{s,t}(u)+\\ell(u^{-1}w)$ because $u^{-1}w=d$ and $\\ell_{s,t}(u)=\\ell(u)$.",
          "step": "8.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 7.1",
            "F5",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-8-2",
          "claim": "**Forward direction of (4).** Let $w\\in W$ and $s\\in S$. By $(\\mathrm P_{\\ell(w)})$ from 7.1, $wC^\\circ\\subseteq B_s$, or $wC^\\circ\\subseteq sB_s$ and $\\ell(sw)=\\ell(w)-1$; in particular $wC^\\circ\\subseteq sB_s$ implies $\\ell(sw)<\\ell(w)$.",
          "step": "8.2",
          "inputs": [
            "step 7.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-9-1",
          "claim": "**Converse direction of (4) and conclusion.** Suppose $\\ell(sw)<\\ell(w)$ and put $w':=sw$, so that $\\ell(w')=\\ell(w)-1$ and $w=sw'$. By $(\\mathrm P_{\\ell(w')})$ from 7.1 applied to $w'$, either $w'C^\\circ\\subseteq B_s$, or $w'C^\\circ\\subseteq sB_s$ and $\\ell(sw')=\\ell(w')-1$; the second alternative would give $\\ell(w)=\\ell(sw')=\\ell(w')-1=\\ell(w)-2$, which is impossible, so $w'C^\\circ\\subseteq B_s$. Applying the linear bijection $f\\mapsto s\\cdot f$ of [F3] gives $wC^\\circ=s(w'C^\\circ)\\subseteq sB_s$. Together with 8.2 this is the equivalence (4); and (1) is 4.1, (2) is 7.1 and (3) is 8.1, so all four clauses of the statement hold.",
