    "thm-cg-root-length-criterion-and-faithfulness": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-cg-rank-two-prefix-and-chamber-length-induction",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $S$ be a finite set, $m$ a Coxeter matrix on $S$, $W$ the group presented by $(S,m)$ with length function $\\ell$ ([[def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length]]), $V=\\mathbb R^S$, $B$ the Coxeter form, $\\rho:W\\to\\mathrm{GL}(V)$ the canonical reflection homomorphism with root system $\\Phi$ ([[def-cg-canonical-reflection-homomorphism]]), and let $V^*$ be the algebraic dual with its dual action, its chamber $C$, its interior $C^\\circ$ and its root hyperplanes $H_\\alpha$ ([[def-cg-dual-chambers-and-reflection-hyperplanes]]). For $s\\in S$ put\n$$B_s:=\\{f\\in V^*:f(e_s)>0\\};$$\nthen $C^\\circ=\\bigcap_{s\\in S}B_s$ and the translate $sB_s=\\{f\\in V^*:f(e_s)<0\\}$ is the opposite open half-space. For distinct $s,t$ let $W_{s,t}:=\\langle s,t\\rangle\\le W$ be the standard parabolic subgroup, with intrinsic length $\\ell_{s,t}$, which agrees with $\\ell$ on $W_{s,t}$ ([[thm-hh-parabolic-minimal-representatives-and-length-additivity]] (2)). For $s\\ne t$ and $k\\ge0$ let $u_k$ be the alternating word $s\\,t\\,s\\cdots$ of $k$ letters beginning with $s$, so $u_0=1$.\n\n**(1) Rank-two half-space alternative.** Let $s\\ne t$ and $v\\in W_{s,t}$. For each $s'\\in\\{s,t\\}$ exactly one of\n$$v(B_s\\cap B_t)\\subseteq B_{s'},\\qquad v(B_s\\cap B_t)\\subseteq s'B_{s'}$$\nholds, and if the second holds then $\\ell_{s,t}(s'v)=\\ell_{s,t}(v)-1$. Moreover, if $m(s,t)<\\infty$ then the elements $u_0,\\dots,u_{2m(s,t)-1}$ exhaust $W_{s,t}$, with $\\ell_{s,t}(u_k)=\\min(k,2m(s,t)-k)$, and the unique element of $W_{s,t}$ having both $s$ and $t$ as left descents is $u_{m(s,t)}$; if $m(s,t)=\\infty$ then $\\ell_{s,t}(u_k)=k$ for every $k\\ge0$, the elements $u_k$ are pairwise distinct, and no element of $W_{s,t}$ has both $s$ and $t$ as left descents.\n\n**(2) The simultaneous induction.** For $n\\ge0$ let\n$$(\\mathrm P_n):\\ \\text{for all }w\\in W\\text{ with }\\ell(w)=n\\text{ and all }s\\in S,\\ wC^\\circ\\subseteq B_s\\text{, or }wC^\\circ\\subseteq sB_s\\text{ and }\\ell(sw)=\\ell(w)-1;$$\n$$(\\mathrm Q_n):\\ \\text{for all }w\\in W\\text{ with }\\ell(w)=n\\text{ and all }s\\ne t\\text{ there is }u\\in W_{s,t}\\text{ with }wC^\\circ\\subseteq u(B_s\\cap B_t)\\text{ and }\\ell(w)=\\ell_{s,t}(u)+\\ell(u^{-1}w).$$\nThen $(\\mathrm P_n)$ and $(\\mathrm Q_n)$ hold for every $n\\ge0$. Explicitly: $(\\mathrm P_0)$ and $(\\mathrm Q_0)$ are immediate; (1) together with $(\\mathrm Q_n)$ yields $(\\mathrm P_{n+1})$; and (1) together with $(\\mathrm Q_n)$ and $(\\mathrm P_m)$ for all $m\\le n+1$ yields $(\\mathrm Q_{n+1})$.\n\n**(3) Canonical factorisation.** Let $w\\in W$ and $s\\ne t$. Write $w=u\\,d$ with $u\\in W_{s,t}$ and $d$ the minimal representative of the left coset $W_{s,t}w$. Then $\\ell(w)=\\ell(u)+\\ell(d)$ and $\\ell(s'd)>\\ell(d)$ for $s'\\in\\{s,t\\}$ ([[thm-hh-parabolic-minimal-representatives-and-length-additivity]] (3)), and\n$$wC^\\circ\\subseteq u(B_s\\cap B_t),\\qquad \\ell(w)=\\ell_{s,t}(u)+\\ell(u^{-1}w).$$\n\n**(4) Chamber-descent equivalence.** For every $w\\in W$ and $s\\in S$ one has $wC^\\circ\\subseteq B_s$ or $wC^\\circ\\subseteq sB_s$; moreover\n$$wC^\\circ\\subseteq sB_s\\iff \\ell(sw)<\\ell(w).$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-cg-root-sign-and-simple-reflection-positivity",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $S$ be a finite set, $m$ a Coxeter matrix, $W$ the presented group ([[def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length]]), $V=\\mathbb R^S$ with Coxeter form $B$, the reflections $r_a$ ([[def-cg-real-coxeter-form-and-reflection]]), the canonical reflection homomorphism $\\rho:W\\to\\mathrm{GL}(V)$, the root system $\\Phi=\\{\\rho(w)e_s\\}$, the reflections $T$, and the positive cone $V_+=\\{\\sum_s\\lambda_se_s:\\lambda_s\\ge0\\}$ ([[def-cg-canonical-reflection-homomorphism]]); every root satisfies $B(\\alpha,\\alpha)=1$ ([[lem-cg-reflection-representation-descends-and-root-norms]] (3)). Let $C,C^\\circ$ be the chamber and its interior of the dual action and put $B_s:=\\{f\\in V^*:f(e_s)>0\\}$ ([[def-cg-dual-chambers-and-reflection-hyperplanes]]).\n\n**(1) Sign criterion for the cone.** For $v\\in V$: $v\\in V_+\\setminus\\{0\\}$ if and only if $f(v)>0$ for every $f\\in C^\\circ$.\n\n**(2) Roots have a sign.** Every root $\\alpha\\in\\Phi$ lies in $V_+\\setminus\\{0\\}$ or in $-V_+\\setminus\\{0\\}$, and not in both. Hence, with\n$$\\Phi_+:=\\Phi\\cap V_+,\\qquad \\Phi_-:=\\Phi\\cap(-V_+),$$\none has $\\Phi=\\Phi_+\\sqcup\\Phi_-$, $\\Phi_-=-\\Phi_+$, $e_s\\in\\Phi_+$ for every $s\\in S$, and for every $w\\in W$ and $s\\in S$\n$$\\rho(w)e_s\\in\\Phi_+\\iff w^{-1}C^\\circ\\subseteq B_s,\\qquad \\rho(w)e_s\\in\\Phi_-\\iff w^{-1}C^\\circ\\subseteq sB_s.$$\nIn particular $f(\\alpha)>0$ for all $f\\in C^\\circ$ when $\\alpha\\in\\Phi_+$, and $f(\\alpha)<0$ for all $f\\in C^\\circ$ when $\\alpha\\in\\Phi_-$.\n\n**(3) Simple reflections act on positive roots.** For every $s\\in S$,\n$$r_s(\\Phi_+\\setminus\\{e_s\\})=\\Phi_+\\setminus\\{e_s\\},\\qquad r_se_s=-e_s,\\qquad r_s\\Phi=\\Phi;$$\nequivalently $r_s\\Phi_+=(\\Phi_+\\setminus\\{e_s\\})\\cup\\{-e_s\\}$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-cg-dual-chambers-and-reflection-hyperplanes",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $S$, $m$, $V$, $B$, $W$, $\\rho$, $\\Phi$ be as in [[def-cg-canonical-reflection-homomorphism]], and let $V^*$ be the algebraic dual of $V$ ([[def-algebraic-dual-and-linear-functional]]).\n\n**(1) The dual action.** For $w\\in W$ and $f\\in V^*$ define $w\\cdot f\\in V^*$ by $$(w\\cdot f)(v):=f(\\rho(w)^{-1}v).$$ This is the dual (contragredient) action of $\\rho$; that it really is a left action by linear maps is proved in [[lem-cg-dual-action-and-chamber-faces-exist]]. **The dual action is used even when $B$ is degenerate**: no identification of $V$ with $V^*$ through $B$ is made or assumed.\n\n**(2) Chambers, faces, root hyperplanes.** The **closed chamber** is $C:=\\{f\\in V^*:f(e_s)\\ge0\\text{ for all }s\\in S\\}$, its **interior** is $C^\\circ:=\\{f\\in V^*:f(e_s)>0\\text{ for all }s\\in S\\}$, and for $I\\subseteq S$ the **face** $C_I$ is $$C_I:=\\{f\\in V^*:f(e_s)=0\\text{ for }s\\in I\\text{ and }f(e_s)>0\\text{ for }s\\notin I\\},$$ the set of functionals of $C$ vanishing exactly on $I$. For a root $\\alpha\\in\\Phi$ the **root hyperplane** is $H_\\alpha:=\\{f\\in V^*:f(\\alpha)=0\\}=\\ker(\\mathrm{ev}_\\alpha)$, the kernel of the evaluation functional $f\\mapsto f(\\alpha)$ ([[def-kernel-and-image-of-a-linear-map]]).\n\nNeither the nonemptiness of the $C_I$ nor any orbit or tiling property is asserted here; existence of the faces and the exact rank-two chamber structure are proved in [[lem-cg-dual-action-and-chamber-faces-exist]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-hh-coxeter-exchange-deletion-and-faithfulness",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(S,m)$ be a finite Coxeter matrix, $W$ the presented group, $\\ell$ its length function, and let $T$, $\\eta$, $\\Phi$ and the right action of $W$ on $\\{\\pm1\\}\\times T$ be as in [[lem-hh-dihedral-root-recurrence-and-root-sign]].\n\n1. **Sign character and parity.** There is a unique homomorphism $\\operatorname{sgn}:W\\to\\{\\pm1\\}$ with $\\operatorname{sgn}(s)=-1$ for all $s\\in S$, and $\\operatorname{sgn}(w)=(-1)^{\\ell(w)}$ for all $w\\in W$. Consequently, for all $w\\in W$ and $s\\in S$,\n$$\\ell(sw)=\\ell(w)\\pm1,\\qquad \\ell(ws)=\\ell(w)\\pm1,$$\nwith $\\ell(sw)\\equiv\\ell(w)+1\\pmod2$ and $\\ell(ws)\\equiv\\ell(w)+1\\pmod2$.\n2. **Exchange.** Let $w=s_1\\cdots s_k$ be a reduced expression and let $s\\in S$ satisfy $\\ell(sw)=k-1$. Then\n$$sw=s_1\\cdots\\widehat{s_i}\\cdots s_k$$\nfor some $i\\in\\{1,\\dots,k\\}$; equivalently, $w$ has a reduced expression beginning with $s$, and $s$ is a prefix reflection of any reduced expression of $w$. Right-handed form: if $\\ell(ws)=k-1$ then $ws=s_1\\cdots\\widehat{s_i}\\cdots s_k$ for some $i$.\n3. **Deletion.** If the word $(s_1,\\dots,s_k)$ in $S$ is not reduced, then there are $i<j$ with\n$$s_1\\cdots\\widehat{s_i}\\cdots\\widehat{s_j}\\cdots s_k=s_1\\cdots s_k .$$\nHence repeated deletion of two letters transforms every word into a reduced expression for the same element, and a word is reduced if and only if it cannot be shortened by deleting two letters.\n4. **Faithfulness of the signed action.** The right action of $W$ on $\\{\\pm1\\}\\times T$ is faithful, so $W$ embeds in $\\operatorname{Sym}(\\{\\pm1\\}\\times T)$; in particular distinct simple generators are distinct in $W$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "1.3",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-hh-parabolic-minimal-representatives-and-length-additivity",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(S,m)$ be a finite Coxeter matrix with presented group $W$, length $\\ell$ and geometric representation $\\sigma:W\\to\\mathrm{GL}_K(E)$ as in [[def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length]], [[def-hh-geometric-coxeter-representation-and-roots]] and [[lem-hh-dihedral-root-recurrence-and-root-sign]], and let $J\\subseteq S$.\n\n1. **Support and reduction.** For every $w\\in W$ the set $S(w)$ of letters occurring in a reduced expression of $w$ is independent of the reduced expression, and every word in $S$ representing $w$ can be transformed into a reduced expression by repeatedly deleting two letters (Tits reduction, using only letters already present). Consequently\n$$W_J=\\langle J\\rangle=\\{w\\in W:S(w)\\subseteq J\\}.$$\n2. **Intrinsic parabolic presentation.** Let $W_J^\\ast$ be the group presented by the restricted Coxeter matrix $(J,m|_J)$ in the sense of [[def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length]]. The canonical homomorphism $W_J^\\ast\\to W_J$, $s\\mapsto s$, is an isomorphism. Hence $(W_J,J)$ is a Coxeter system, its intrinsic length function $\\ell_J$ agrees with the ambient length $\\ell$ on $W_J$, and $W_J\\cap S=J$.\n3. **Minimal coset representatives.** Every left coset $W_J a:=\\{ua:u\\in W_J\\}$ ($a\\in W$, [[def-coset]]) has a unique element $d$ of minimal length; it is characterized by $\\ell(sd)>\\ell(d)$ for all $s\\in J$, and it satisfies\n$$\\ell(ud)=\\ell(u)+\\ell(d)\\qquad\\text{for all }u\\in W_J .$$\nEquivalently, every $w\\in W$ has a unique factorization $w=u\\,d$ with $u\\in W_J$ and $d$ the minimal representative of the left coset $W_J d$, and then $\\ell(w)=\\ell(u)+\\ell(d)$. By inversion ($w\\mapsto w^{-1}$ preserves lengths and interchanges the two coset families $\\{W_Ja\\}$ and $\\{aW_J\\}$), every right coset $aW_J:=\\{au:u\\in W_J\\}$ has a unique minimal element $d$, characterized by $\\ell(ds)>\\ell(d)$ for all $s\\in J$ and satisfying $\\ell(du)=\\ell(d)+\\ell(u)$ for all $u\\in W_J$.\n4. **Type A.** Let $n\\ge2$, $S=\\{s_1,\\dots,s_{n-1}\\}$ and $m(s_i,s_j):=3$ if $|i-j|=1$, $m(s_i,s_j):=2$ if $|i-j|>1$ (a Coxeter matrix of type $A_{n-1}$). Then $s_i\\mapsto(i\\ i+1)$ extends to an isomorphism $W\\to S_n$ (the letters $1,\\dots,n$ carry the library's symmetric group by the order-preserving identification with $\\{0,\\dots,n-1\\}$, under which $(i\\ i+1)$ is the adjacent transposition $(i-1\\ i)$), and for every $w\\in W$,\n$$\\ell(w)=\\operatorname{inv}\\bigl(\\varphi(w)\\bigr),$$\nthe inversion number of the corresponding permutation ([[def-inversions-inversion-number-and-sign]]). In particular a word in the $s_i$ is reduced if and only if its length equals the inversion number of its value.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "1.3",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-cg-dual-chambers-and-reflection-hyperplanes",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $S$, $m$, $V$, $B$, $W$, $\\rho$, $\\Phi$ be as in [[def-cg-canonical-reflection-homomorphism]], and let $V^*$ be the algebraic dual of $V$ ([[def-algebraic-dual-and-linear-functional]]).\n\n**(1) The dual action.** For $w\\in W$ and $f\\in V^*$ define $w\\cdot f\\in V^*$ by $$(w\\cdot f)(v):=f(\\rho(w)^{-1}v).$$ This is the dual (contragredient) action of $\\rho$; that it really is a left action by linear maps is proved in [[lem-cg-dual-action-and-chamber-faces-exist]]. **The dual action is used even when $B$ is degenerate**: no identification of $V$ with $V^*$ through $B$ is made or assumed.\n\n**(2) Chambers, faces, root hyperplanes.** The **closed chamber** is $C:=\\{f\\in V^*:f(e_s)\\ge0\\text{ for all }s\\in S\\}$, its **interior** is $C^\\circ:=\\{f\\in V^*:f(e_s)>0\\text{ for all }s\\in S\\}$, and for $I\\subseteq S$ the **face** $C_I$ is $$C_I:=\\{f\\in V^*:f(e_s)=0\\text{ for }s\\in I\\text{ and }f(e_s)>0\\text{ for }s\\notin I\\},$$ the set of functionals of $C$ vanishing exactly on $I$. For a root $\\alpha\\in\\Phi$ the **root hyperplane** is $H_\\alpha:=\\{f\\in V^*:f(\\alpha)=0\\}=\\ker(\\mathrm{ev}_\\alpha)$, the kernel of the evaluation functional $f\\mapsto f(\\alpha)$ ([[def-kernel-and-image-of-a-linear-map]]).\n\nNeither the nonemptiness of the $C_I$ nor any orbit or tiling property is asserted here; existence of the faces and the exact rank-two chamber structure are proved in [[lem-cg-dual-action-and-chamber-faces-exist]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.3",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-cg-dual-action-and-chamber-faces-exist",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "**(1) The dual action is an action by linear maps.** For all $w_1,w_2\\in W$ and $f\\in V^*$ one has $\\mathrm{id}\\cdot f=f$ and $w_1\\cdot(w_2\\cdot f)=(w_1w_2)\\cdot f$, and for each $w$ the map $f\\mapsto w\\cdot f$ is linear.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.3",
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "**Set-up.** Recall from [F2] that $C^\\circ\\subseteq B_s$ and $B_s\\cap sB_s=\\emptyset$ for every $s\\in S$, and that the two sign equivalences of the root system hold; from [F4] that every $w$ acts on $V^*$ as a bijection with inverse the action of $w^{-1}$; and from [F1] that for every $x\\in W$ and every generator $s$ exactly one of $xC^\\circ\\subseteq B_s$, $xC^\\circ\\subseteq sB_s$ holds.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "given"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "**The two-sided root-length criterion.** Fix $w\\in W$ and $s\\in S$. By [F2], $\\rho(w)e_s\\in\\Phi_-$ if and only if $w^{-1}C^\\circ\\subseteq sB_s$; by [F1] applied to the element $w^{-1}$ this holds if and only if $\\ell(sw^{-1})<\\ell(w^{-1})$. Since $(sw^{-1})^{-1}=ws$ and $\\ell$ is inversion invariant, [F3] gives $\\ell(sw^{-1})=\\ell(ws)$ and $\\ell(w^{-1})=\\ell(w)$, so $\\rho(w)e_s\\in\\Phi_-\\iff\\ell(ws)<\\ell(w)$. Finally $\\ell(ws)=\\ell(w)\\pm1$ by [F3] and $\\Phi=\\Phi_+\\sqcup\\Phi_-$ by [F2], so this is equivalent to the statement that $\\ell(ws)>\\ell(w)$ if and only if $\\rho(w)e_s\\in\\Phi_+$; both claims of (1) follow.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-3",
          "claim": "**Disjoint chambers.** Suppose $wC^\\circ\\cap C^\\circ\\ne\\emptyset$ and $w\\ne1$. Choose a reduced expression of $w$ and let $s$ be its first letter, so that $w=sw'$ with $\\ell(w')=\\ell(w)-1$ by [F3]. The intersection point lies in $C^\\circ\\subseteq B_s$, so $wC^\\circ$ meets $B_s$, whence $wC^\\circ\\not\\subseteq sB_s$ because $B_s\\cap sB_s=\\emptyset$; applying the bijection $f\\mapsto s\\cdot f$ from [F4] and using that its square is the identity gives $w'C^\\circ\\not\\subseteq B_s$. By [F1] applied to $w'$, therefore $w'C^\\circ\\subseteq sB_s$ and $\\ell(sw')=\\ell(w')-1$; but $sw'=w$, so $\\ell(w)=\\ell(w')-1=\\ell(w)-2$, a contradiction. Hence $w=1$; and the stated equivalence holds because $vC^\\circ\\cap wC^\\circ\\ne\\emptyset$ if and only if $(w^{-1}v)C^\\circ\\cap C^\\circ\\ne\\emptyset$ by [F4].",
          "step": "1.3",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "**Faithfulness.** If $\\rho(w)=\\mathrm{id}_V$, then $\\rho(w)^{-1}=\\mathrm{id}_V$ as well, so $(w\\cdot f)(v)=f(\\rho(w)^{-1}v)=f(v)$ for all $f\\in V^*$ and $v\\in V$ by [F4]; hence $w\\cdot f=f$ for every $f$ and $wC^\\circ=C^\\circ$ meets $C^\\circ$, so 1.3 gives $w=1$. If the dual action of $w$ is the identity, then for any $f\\in C^\\circ$ one has $f=w\\cdot f\\in wC^\\circ\\cap C^\\circ$, which is nonempty, and 1.3 again gives $w=1$. Finally let $w\\ne1$ and let $s$ be the last letter of a reduced expression of $w$, so that $\\ell(ws)=\\ell(w)-1$ by [F3]; by the criterion of 1.2, $\\rho(w)e_s\\in\\Phi_-$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.2",
            "step 1.3",
            "F3",
            "F4"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "For w=1 the criterion of step 1.2 applies with w^{-1}=1\u2208W, and step 1.3 uses the hypothesis w\u22601 only to pick a first letter of a reduced expression, so the empty case \u2113(w)=0 satisfies \u03c1(1)e_s=e_s\u2208\u03a6_+."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Step 1.1 records that every root is nonzero, and the zero-length case is the base of step 1.2; the criterion at \u2113(w)=0 reads \u2113(s)>0 with \u03c1(1)e_s=e_s\u2208\u03a6_+, matching the statement."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "The length difference \u2113(ws)\u2212\u2113(w)=\u00b11 is the content of step 1.2, where the two alternatives are matched with \u03a6_\u00b1; the case \u2113(ws)=\u2113(w)+1 is the first equivalence, \u2113(ws)=\u2113(w)\u22121 the second."
        },
        {
          "case": "degenerate",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Faithfulness is proved for every finite-rank Coxeter matrix with B possibly degenerate or indefinite: step 1.2 uses only the sign partition and the chamber equivalence, and steps 1.3 and 2.1 use only chamber disjointness, never definiteness of B."
        },
        {
          "case": "endpoints",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "No interval or ordered family is quantified over: the two-sided criterion (1) and the statements (2)-(3) are indexed by w\u2208W and s\u2208S only, so there is no endpoint case."
        },
        {
          "case": "nonempty-choice",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "Choice-free: the argument uses C\u00b0\u2260\u2205 by a constructed element, the unique inverse w^{-1}, and first and last letters of a given reduced expression; no selection from an infinite family occurs."
        },
