        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-of-square-roots",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $F$ be a complete ordered field ([[def-complete-ordered-field]]). Then every\n$a \\in F$ with $a \\ge 0$ has a **unique** $s \\in F$ with $s \\ge 0$ and $s^2 = a$;\nwe write $s = \\sqrt{a}$. Consequently the positive elements of $F$ are exactly the\nnonzero squares: $x > 0$ if and only if $x = y^2$ for some $y \\neq 0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.3"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "**The case $A_2$: the constant.** Put $x:=\\pi/3$, $q:=\\cos x$ and $r:=\\sin x$. The addition formulas give $\\cos 2x=\\cos^2x-\\sin^2x=2q^2-1$ and $\\sin 2x=2qr$, hence $\\cos 3x=\\cos(2x+x)=(2q^2-1)q-(2qr)r=2q^3-q-2q(1-q^2)=4q^3-3q$; since $3x=\\pi$ and $\\cos\\pi=-1$, we get $4q^3-3q+1=0$, that is $(q+1)(2q-1)^2=0$. Now $0<\\pi/3<\\pi/2<\\pi$ and cosine is strictly decreasing on $[0,\\pi]$ with $\\cos 0=1$ and $\\cos(\\pi/2)=0$, so $0<q<1$; hence $q\\ne-1$ and $q=1/2$. Thus in this case $c=1/2$, and the reflection formulas of [F1] give $\\rho(s)e_t=e_t+e_s$ and $\\rho(t)e_s=e_s+e_t$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F7",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "**The case $I_2(5)$: the chambers.** By [F6] the ten chambers $u_kC^\\circ$ $(0\\le k<10)$ are exactly the ten sectors cut out by the five root lines. By [F5], $(st)^5=1$ together with $s^2=t^2=1$ gives $su_k=u_{1-k}$ and $tu_k=u_{-1-k}$ with indices modulo $10$; combined with $\\ell(u_k)=\\min(k,10-k)$ this gives $\\ell(su_k)=\\ell(u_k)-1$ exactly for $1\\le k\\le5$, and $\\ell(tu_k)=\\ell(u_k)-1$ exactly for $5\\le k\\le9$. By the shortening equivalence of [F6], the chambers on the negative side of $H_{e_s}$ are exactly $u_1C^\\circ,\\dots,u_5C^\\circ$ and those on the negative side of $H_{e_t}$ exactly $u_5C^\\circ,\\dots,u_9C^\\circ$.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-3",
          "claim": "**The case $I_2(\\infty)$: the invariant functional direction.** Here $c=1$, so $B(u,u)=B(e_s,e_s)+2B(e_s,e_t)+B(e_t,e_t)=1-2+1=0$, $B(u,e_s)=B(e_s,e_s)+B(e_t,e_s)=1-1=0$ and $B(u,e_t)=B(e_s,e_t)+B(e_t,e_t)=-1+1=0$, where $u:=e_s+e_t$; by bilinearity $B(u,\\cdot)=0$ on $\\mathbb Re_s+\\mathbb Re_t$. Moreover $\\rho(s)u=\\rho(s)e_s+\\rho(s)e_t=-e_s+(e_t+2e_s)=u$ and $\\rho(t)u=(e_s+2e_t)-e_t=u$, so $\\rho(w)u=u$ for every $w\\in W$, because $W$ is generated by $s,t$ and $\\rho$ is a homomorphism.",
          "step": "1.3",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F5",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "**The case $A_2$: the positive roots.** By 1.1, the three vectors $e_s=\\rho(1)e_s$, $e_t=\\rho(1)e_t$ and $e_s+e_t=\\rho(t)e_s$ are roots; they lie in $V_+\\setminus\\{0\\}$, hence in $\\Phi_+$, and they are pairwise distinct as functions on $\\{s,t\\}$, with values $(1,0)$, $(0,1)$, $(1,1)$. By [F6] the six chambers are cut out by the three root hyperplanes $H_\\beta$, $\\beta\\in\\Phi$; the map $\\alpha\\mapsto H_\\alpha$ from $\\Phi_+$ onto these lines is injective, because two roots on one line are proportional and both have $B$-norm one by [F2], forcing the proportionality factor to be $\\pm1$, and it is surjective because every $H_\\beta$ equals $H_{-\\beta}$ with exactly one of $\\pm\\beta$ in $\\Phi_+$. Hence $|\\Phi_+|=3$ and $\\Phi_+=\\{e_s,\\,e_t,\\,e_s+e_t\\}$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F6",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "claim": "**The case $A_2$: the chambers.** Write $y_s:=f(e_s)$ and $y_t:=f(e_t)$ for $f\\in V^*$, so that $f$ is described by the pair $(y_s,y_t)$. By [F6], $f\\in wC^\\circ$ if and only if $f(\\rho(w)e_s)>0$ and $f(\\rho(w)e_t)>0$. Using the products $\\rho(s)e_s=-e_s$, $\\rho(s)e_t=e_s+e_t$, $\\rho(t)e_t=-e_t$, $\\rho(t)e_s=e_s+e_t$ from [F1] and 1.1, together with $\\rho(st)e_s=e_t$, $\\rho(st)e_t=-e_s-e_t$, $\\rho(ts)e_s=-e_s-e_t$, $\\rho(ts)e_t=e_s$, $\\rho(sts)e_s=-e_t$, $\\rho(sts)e_t=-e_s$ (computed from $\\rho(uv)=\\rho(u)\\rho(v)$), this gives $C^\\circ=\\{y_s>0,\\ y_t>0\\}$, $sC^\\circ=\\{y_s<0,\\ y_s+y_t>0\\}$, $stC^\\circ=\\{y_t>0,\\ y_s+y_t<0\\}$, $stsC^\\circ=\\{y_s<0,\\ y_t<0\\}=-C^\\circ$, $tsC^\\circ=\\{y_s>0,\\ y_s+y_t<0\\}$ and $tC^\\circ=\\{y_t<0,\\ y_s+y_t>0\\}$. On each of these six sets the signs of $(y_s,\\,y_t,\\,y_s+y_t)$ are the six distinct patterns $(+,+,+)$, $(-,+,+)$, $(-,+,-)$, $(-,-,-)$, $(+,-,-)$, $(+,-,+)$; these are exactly the six combinations not excluded by one of the three equations $y_s=0$, $y_t=0$, $y_s+y_t=0$, so the six chambers are the six sectors, in the stated cyclic order. By [F5], $s^2=t^2=1$ and $(st)^3=1$ give $s(st)^j=(ts)^js$ and $t(st)^j=(ts)^jt$ for all $j\\ge0$ and $(ts)^j=(st)^{-j}$, whence $su_k=u_{1-k}$ and $tu_k=u_{-1-k}$ with indices read modulo $6$; combined with $\\ell(u_k)=\\min(k,6-k)$ this gives $\\ell(su_k)=\\ell(u_k)-1$ exactly for $k\\in\\{1,2,3\\}$ and $\\ell(tu_k)=\\ell(u_k)-1$ exactly for $k\\in\\{3,4,5\\}$. By the shortening equivalence of [F6], $H_{e_s}$ separates $C^\\circ$ from $u_1C^\\circ,u_2C^\\circ,u_3C^\\circ$ and $H_{e_t}$ from $u_3C^\\circ,u_4C^\\circ,u_5C^\\circ$.",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra",
            "F1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-3",
          "claim": "**The case $I_2(5)$: the constant.** Put $c:=\\cos(\\pi/5)$ (the constant of the form at $m=5$) and $q:=\\cos(2\\pi/5)$. By 1.1, $\\cos(\\pi/3)=1/2$; since $0<\\pi/5<\\pi/3<\\pi$ and cosine is strictly decreasing on $[0,\\pi]$, we have $c>1/2>0$. The double-angle formula gives $q=2c^2-1$, and since $4\\pi/5=\\pi-\\pi/5$ with $\\cos(\\pi-x)=\\cos((-x)+\\pi)=-\\cos(-x)=-\\cos x$, we get $\\cos(4\\pi/5)=-c$; on the other hand $\\cos(4\\pi/5)=2q^2-1=2(2c^2-1)^2-1$. Hence $2(2c^2-1)^2-1+c=0$, that is $8c^4-8c^2+c+1=0$, and because $8c^4-8c^2+c+1=(2c-1)(c+1)(4c^2-2c-1)$ we obtain $4c^2-2c-1=0$, as $c>1/2$ and $c\\ne-1$. Finally $4X^2-2X-1=4(X-\\tfrac{1+\\sqrt5}{4})(X-\\tfrac{1-\\sqrt5}{4})$ by expansion, so $c$ is one of the two numbers $\\tfrac{1\\pm\\sqrt5}{4}$; since $\\sqrt5>1$ gives $\\tfrac{1-\\sqrt5}{4}<0<c$, we conclude $c=\\tfrac{1+\\sqrt5}{4}=\\phi/2$ with $\\phi:=\\tfrac{1+\\sqrt5}{2}$, and then $2c=\\phi$ and $4c^2-1=2c+1-1=2c=\\phi$.",
          "step": "2.3",
          "inputs": [
            "F7",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-4",
          "claim": "**The case $I_2(\\infty)$: the positive roots.** Put $\\Psi:=\\{e_s+ku:k\\ge0\\}\\cup\\{e_t+ku:k\\ge0\\}$. First, $\\Psi\\subseteq\\Phi_+$: clearly $e_s,e_t\\in\\Psi\\cap\\Phi_+$, and for $k\\ge1$ the identities $e_s+ku=\\rho(s)(e_t+(k-1)u)$ and $e_t+ku=\\rho(t)(e_s+(k-1)u)$ hold by 1.3, so induction on $k$ shows that every element of $\\Psi$ is a root; every element of $\\Psi$ has nonnegative coefficients in $e_s,e_t$, so $\\Psi\\subseteq\\Phi\\cap V_+\\setminus\\{0\\}=\\Phi_+$. Conversely, $\\Psi\\cup(-\\Psi)$ is stable under $\\rho(s)$ and $\\rho(t)$: by 1.3 one has $\\rho(s)(e_s+ku)=-e_s+ku$, which is $e_t+(k-1)u$ for $k\\ge1$ and $-e_s$ for $k=0$; $\\rho(s)(e_t+ku)=(e_t+2e_s)+ku=e_s+(k+1)u$; $\\rho(t)(e_t+ku)=-e_t+ku$, which is $e_s+(k-1)u$ for $k\\ge1$ and $-e_t$ for $k=0$; and $\\rho(t)(e_s+ku)=(e_s+2e_t)+ku=e_t+(k+1)u$. Since $W$ is generated by $s,t$ and $\\rho$ is a homomorphism, $\\Psi\\cup(-\\Psi)$ is stable under every $\\rho(w)$; it contains $e_s,e_t$, so it contains the orbit $\\Phi$. Hence $\\Phi\\subseteq\\Psi\\cup(-\\Psi)$, and an element of $\\Phi_+$ lies in $\\Psi$: it lies in $\\Psi\\cup(-\\Psi)$, and no element of $-\\Psi$ has all coefficients nonnegative, while the elements of $V_+$ do. Therefore $\\Phi_+=\\Psi$; finally the two families $e_s+ku$ and $e_t+ku$ are disjoint and each is injective in $k$, because $u\\ne0$ while evaluating $e_s+ku=e_s+k'u$ at $s$ gives $k=k'$, and $e_s+ku=e_t+k'u$ would give $1+k=k'$ at $s$ and $k=1+k'$ at $t$, hence $k=k+2$, a contradiction; so $\\Phi_+$ is infinite.",
          "step": "2.4",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "step 1.3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-5",
          "claim": "**The case $I_2(\\infty)$: the inversion sets.** By [F5] the $u_k$ are pairwise distinct with $\\ell(u_k)=k$, and $u_{2k}=(st)^k$, $u_{2k+1}=(st)^ks$. We prove by induction on $k\\ge0$ the two formulas $N((st)^k)=\\{ju+e_t:0\\le j\\le 2k-1\\}$ and $N((st)^ks)=\\{ju+e_s:0\\le j\\le 2k\\}$. For $k=0$ the first is $N(1)=\\emptyset$ and the second is $N(s)=\\{e_s\\}$. Assume the second formula for some $k\\ge0$. Since $\\ell((st)^{k+1})=2k+2>2k+1=\\ell((st)^ks)$, the recursion of [F3] gives $N((st)^{k+1})=\\{e_t\\}\\sqcup\\rho(t)N((st)^ks)$; by 1.3, $\\rho(t)e_s=e_s+2e_t=u+e_t$, so $\\rho(t)\\{ju+e_s:0\\le j\\le2k\\}=\\{ju+u+e_t:0\\le j\\le2k\\}=\\{(j+1)u+e_t:0\\le j\\le2k\\}$ and therefore $N((st)^{k+1})=\\{ju+e_t:0\\le j\\le2k+1\\}$, which is the first formula with $k+1$. Since $\\ell((st)^{k+1}s)=2k+3>2k+2=\\ell((st)^{k+1})$, again, but with $\\rho(s)e_t=e_t+2e_s=u+e_s$ from 1.3, $N((st)^{k+1}s)=\\{e_s\\}\\sqcup\\rho(s)\\{ju+e_t:0\\le j\\le2k+1\\}=\\{ju+e_s:0\\le j\\le2k+2\\}$, the second formula with $k+1$. So both formulas hold for all $k\\ge0$. Their elements are pairwise distinct because $u\\ne0$, so the two sets have cardinalities $2k$ and $2k+1$, equal to $\\ell((st)^k)=2k$ and $\\ell((st)^ks)=2k+1$ by [F5], in agreement with [F4].",
          "step": "2.5",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "step 1.3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "**The case $A_2$: the inversion sets.** By [F3] and 1.1, and since $\\ell(u_0)=0<\\ell(u_1)=1<\\ell(u_2)=2<\\ell(u_3)=3$ by [F5], one has $N(s)=\\{e_s\\}\\sqcup\\rho(s)N(1)=\\{e_s\\}$, $N(t)=\\{e_t\\}$, $N(st)=\\{e_t\\}\\sqcup\\rho(t)N(s)=\\{e_t,\\,e_s+e_t\\}$, $N(ts)=\\{e_s\\}\\sqcup\\rho(s)N(t)=\\{e_s,\\,e_s+e_t\\}$ and $N(sts)=\\{e_s\\}\\sqcup\\rho(s)N(st)=\\{e_s,\\,e_s+e_t,\\,\\rho(s)(e_s+e_t)\\}=\\{e_s,\\,e_s+e_t,\\,e_t\\}=\\Phi_+$, where we used $\\rho(s)e_t=e_s+e_t$ and $\\rho(s)(e_s+e_t)=\\rho(s)e_s+\\rho(s)e_t=-e_s+(e_s+e_t)=e_t$; also $N(1)=\\emptyset$. The cardinalities $0,1,1,2,2,3$ equal $\\ell(1),\\ell(s),\\ell(t),\\ell(st),\\ell(ts),\\ell(sts)$ by [F5], as asserted.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "F5",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-2",
          "claim": "**The case $I_2(5)$: the positive roots.** By 2.3 and [F1], $\\rho(s)e_t=e_t+\\phi e_s$ and $\\rho(t)e_s=e_s+\\phi e_t$, and using $\\rho(uv)=\\rho(u)\\rho(v)$ and $\\phi^2=\\phi+1$ one computes $\\rho(st)e_s=\\rho(s)(e_s+\\phi e_t)=-e_s+\\phi(e_t+\\phi e_s)=(\\phi^2-1)e_s+\\phi e_t=\\phi(e_s+e_t)$. Hence the five vectors $e_s=\\rho(1)e_s$, $e_t=\\rho(1)e_t$, $e_t+\\phi e_s=\\rho(s)e_t$, $e_s+\\phi e_t=\\rho(t)e_s$ and $\\phi(e_s+e_t)=\\rho(st)e_s$ are roots; each lies in $V_+\\setminus\\{0\\}$, so all five lie in $\\Phi_+$, and they are pairwise distinct as functions on $\\{s,t\\}$, with values $(1,0)$, $(0,1)$, $(\\phi,1)$, $(1,\\phi)$, $(\\phi,\\phi)$. By [F6] the ten chambers are cut out by the five root hyperplanes $H_\\beta$, $\\beta\\in\\Phi$, and as in 2.1 the map $\\alpha\\mapsto H_\\alpha$ is a bijection from $\\Phi_+$ onto these lines, so $|\\Phi_+|=5$ and the five listed vectors exhaust $\\Phi_+$; each of them has $B$-norm one because it is a root, by [F2].",
          "step": "3.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F6",
            "step 2.3",
            "algebra",
            "step 2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "**The case $I_2(5)$: the inversion sets $N(u_k)$, $1\\le k\\le5$.** By [F5], $\\ell(u_k)=k$ for $1\\le k\\le5$, so each step below is a shortening-free step of the recursion of [F3] and the union is disjoint. Starting from $N(1)=\\emptyset$ and using the reflection values $\\rho(t)e_s=e_s+\\phi e_t$, $\\rho(s)e_t=e_t+\\phi e_s$, $\\rho(s)(e_s+\\phi e_t)=\\phi(e_s+e_t)$, $\\rho(t)(e_t+\\phi e_s)=\\phi(e_s+e_t)$, $\\rho(t)\\phi(e_s+e_t)=\\phi e_s+e_t$ and $\\rho(s)\\phi(e_s+e_t)=e_s+\\phi e_t$ (all from [F1], $\\rho(uv)=\\rho(u)\\rho(v)$ and $\\phi^2=\\phi+1$) one computes $N(u_1)=\\{e_s\\}$, $N(u_2)=\\{e_t\\}\\sqcup\\rho(t)N(u_1)=\\{e_t,\\ e_s+\\phi e_t\\}$, $N(u_3)=\\{e_s\\}\\sqcup\\rho(s)N(u_2)=\\{e_s,\\ e_t+\\phi e_s,\\ \\phi(e_s+e_t)\\}$, $N(u_4)=\\{e_t\\}\\sqcup\\rho(t)N(u_3)=\\{e_t,\\ e_s+\\phi e_t,\\ \\phi(e_s+e_t),\\ e_t+\\phi e_s\\}$ and $N(u_5)=\\{e_s\\}\\sqcup\\rho(s)N(u_4)=\\{e_s,\\ e_t+\\phi e_s,\\ \\phi(e_s+e_t),\\ e_s+\\phi e_t,\\ e_t\\}=\\Phi_+$, the last equality by 3.2. This gives the displayed sets $N(u_1),\\dots,N(u_5)$, of cardinalities $k=|N(u_k)|=\\ell(u_k)$ as required by [F4].",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
