          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "claim": "**The case $I_2(5)$: the inversion sets $N(u_k)$, $5\\le k<10$.** Since $N(u_5)=\\Phi_+$ by 4.1, the reflection $\\rho(u_5)$ maps $\\Phi_+$ bijectively onto $\\Phi_-$ (as $N(u_5)=\\Phi_+$ means $\\rho(u_5)\\Phi_+\\subseteq\\Phi_-$, and $\\rho(u_5)$ induces a bijection of $\\Phi$); hence for $\\alpha\\in\\Phi_+$: $\\alpha\\in N(u_5w)$ if and only if $\\rho(w)\\alpha\\in\\Phi_+$, because $\\rho(u_5)$ maps $\\Phi_+$ onto $\\Phi_-$ and $\\Phi_-$ onto $\\Phi_+$. Comparing with the definition of $N(w)$ this gives $N(u_5w)=\\Phi_+\\setminus N(w)$ for every $w\\in W$. For $5\\le k<10$ one has $u_k=u_5\\,u_{k-5}'$, where $u_j'$ is the alternating word of length $j$ beginning with $t$; hence $N(u_k)=\\Phi_+\\setminus N(u_{k-5}')$. In particular $N(u_6)=\\Phi_+\\setminus\\{e_t\\}$ and, using the recursion of [F3] once more for $u_2'=ts$ (read with the roles of $s$ and $t$ exchanged, $\\ell(u_2')=2>\\ell(t)=1$), $N(u_2')=\\{e_s\\}\\sqcup\\rho(s)N(u_1')=\\{e_s,\\ e_t+\\phi e_s\\}$, so that $N(u_7)=\\Phi_+\\setminus N(u_2')=\\{e_t,\\ e_s+\\phi e_t,\\ \\phi(e_s+e_t)\\}$, of cardinality $3=\\ell(u_7)$.",
          "step": "5.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "F5",
            "step 4.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-6-1",
          "claim": "**The case $I_2(\\infty)$: the chambers.** Let $\\delta(f):=f(e_s)+f(e_t)$ for $f\\in V^*$, so that $\\delta(f)=f(u)$; since $\\rho(w)u=u$ for all $w$ by 1.3, the value $\\delta(f)$ is preserved by the dual action: $\\delta(w\\cdot f)=(w\\cdot f)(u)=f(\\rho(w)^{-1}u)=f(u)=\\delta(f)$. On $L:=\\{f:\\delta(f)=1\\}$ with coordinate $y_s=f(e_s)$, $C^\\circ$ has trace $(0,1)$. By [F1] and the dual action in [F6], $s$ acts by $y_s\\mapsto-y_s$ and $t$ by $y_s\\mapsto2-y_s$; hence $st$ acts by translation $y_s\\mapsto y_s-2$. It follows that $(st)^kC^\\circ\\cap L=(-2k,1-2k)$ and $(st)^ksC^\\circ\\cap L=(-2k-1,-2k)$ for every integer $k$. These are all intervals $(j,j+1)$, and all group elements are alternating words by [F5] (cancelling adjacent equal letters); therefore these are exactly the open chamber traces, and no integer point belongs to one. Every chamber is a nonempty cone contained in $\\{\\delta>0\\}$, since this holds for $C^\\circ$ and $\\delta$ is invariant. Each chamber $K$ equals the cone $\\{\\lambda x:\\lambda>0,\\ x\\in K\\cap L\\}$ over its trace, because for $f\\in K$ its normalisation $f/\\delta(f)$ lies in $K\\cap L$ and $f=\\delta(f)\\,(f/\\delta(f))$ with $\\delta(f)>0$; hence the chambers are exactly the cones over the intervals $(j,j+1)$. Finally $H_{e_s}\\cap L$ is the point with $y_s=0$, and $H_{e_t}\\cap L$ is the point with $y_s=1$, because $y_t=1-y_s$ on $L$. Together with 1.1-1.3, 2.1-2.5, 3.1-3.2, 4.1 and 5.1 this verifies all the claims of (i), (ii) and (iii).",
